## Overview

The Rust GLM tool parser removes leading and trailing whitespace from generated string arguments before returning them as tool calls. A tool receiving `"    return 1\n"` gets `"return 1"`. For coding agents, this can change written code or break whitespace-sensitive edit matching even when the model supplied the correct text. Preserve the original string, as the Python GLM parser already does after [#42026](https://github.com/vllm-project/vllm/pull/42026).

For example, an edit targeting the complete line `"    total = 1\n"` can instead match inside `subtotal = 10` once the whitespace is removed. The reproducer below shows the parser turning an intended `total = 2` edit into `subtotal = 20`.

**Scope:** This defect was isolated directly in the Rust parser while investigating GLM verification failures. Any model using the Rust frontend's `glm45` or `glm47` tool parser can encounter it when generated string arguments contain leading or trailing whitespace. Both parsers share the affected XML value parser, so the fix applies across models using either parser, for complete and streaming input. Other Rust parsers and the separate Python frontend are outside this patch.

## Claims

- Preserve string arguments in the Rust `glm45` and `glm47` parsers, for complete and streaming input.
- Keep schema-based numeric conversion; the reproducer's `" 2 "` still becomes integer `2`.

## Validation

CPU reproductions against bases `aee8fe202a2f` and `3a6963664537`; the full parser-crate suite was rerun on the submitted patch based on the latter:

| Check | Before | After |
| --- | --- | --- |
| Full-line edit, GLM-4.5 / GLM-4.7, complete / 7-byte chunks (4 combinations) | Changes the wrong variable; returns 21 | Changes only the target line; returns 12 |
| Integer argument in those calls | `2` | `2` |
| Separate indented-code insertion, same 4 parser/chunk combinations | `IndentationError` in all 4 | Valid code returning 7 in all 4 |
| Two new regression tests | Both fail | Both pass |
| Parser crate unit suite | — | 542 passed, 0 failed |

### Minimal reproduction: real Rust parser, no model or GPU

Run from a vLLM checkout with Rust and a C toolchain installed (Linux also needs `pkg-config` and OpenSSL development headers). Verified on Linux CPU with Rust 1.98.0.

<details>
<summary>Copy and run the standalone Cargo example</summary>

```bash
mre_dir="$(mktemp -d)"
mkdir -p "$mre_dir/src"
cat > "$mre_dir/Cargo.toml" <<EOF
[package]
name = "glm-whitespace-mre"
version = "0.1.0"
edition = "2024"

[dependencies]
vllm-parser = { path = "$PWD/rust/src/parser" }
serde_json = "1"
EOF
cat > "$mre_dir/src/main.rs" <<'RS'
use serde_json::{Value, json};
use vllm_parser::tool::{
    Glm45MoeToolParser, Glm47MoeToolParser, Tool, ToolParser, ToolParserOutput,
};

fn main() {
    let old = "    total = 1\n";
    let new = "    total = 2\n";
    let source = "def calculate():\n    subtotal = 10\n    total = 1\n    return subtotal + total\n";
    let expected = source.replacen(old, new, 1);
    let tools = [Tool {
        name: "edit_code".into(),
        description: None,
        parameters: json!({"type": "object", "properties": {
            "old_string": {"type": "string"},
            "new_string": {"type": "string"},
            "count": {"type": "integer"}
        }}),
        strict: None,
    }];
    let text = format!(
        "<tool_call>edit_code\n<arg_key>old_string</arg_key><arg_value>{old}</arg_value>\n<arg_key>new_string</arg_key><arg_value>{new}</arg_value>\n<arg_key>count</arg_key><arg_value> 2 </arg_value></tool_call>"
    );
    let mut all_preserved = true;
    for chunk_size in [text.len(), 7] {
        for (name, mut parser) in [
            ("glm45", Glm45MoeToolParser::create(&tools).unwrap()),
            ("glm47", Glm47MoeToolParser::create(&tools).unwrap()),
        ] {
            let mut output = ToolParserOutput::default();
            for chunk in text.as_bytes().chunks(chunk_size) {
                parser
                    .parse_into(std::str::from_utf8(chunk).unwrap(), &mut output)
                    .unwrap();
            }
            output.append(parser.finish().unwrap());
            let output = output.coalesce();
            let args: Value = serde_json::from_str(&output.calls()[0].arguments).unwrap();
            let actual_old = args["old_string"].as_str().unwrap();
            let actual_new = args["new_string"].as_str().unwrap();
            let edited = source.replacen(actual_old, actual_new, 1);
            println!(
                "{name} chunk_size={chunk_size}: old_string={actual_old:?}, edited={edited:?}, count={}", args["count"]
            );
            all_preserved &= edited == expected && actual_old == old && actual_new == new && args["count"] == 2;
        }
    }
    assert!(all_preserved, "String whitespace was changed");
}
RS
cargo run --manifest-path "$mre_dir/Cargo.toml"
```

The original code changes `subtotal = 10` to `subtotal = 20`, leaves `total = 1` unchanged, and fails the assertion. The patch changes only the intended line to `total = 2` in all four combinations. The numeric argument remains integer `2`.

</details>

Regression command, from the repository root:

```bash
cargo test --locked --manifest-path rust/Cargo.toml -p vllm-parser --lib
```

